1 NOTE {geni:about_me} =Esper Alekseevich Ukhtomsky=
2 CONT
2 CONT Prince '''Esper Alekseevich Ukhtomsky''' (1834-1885) is a Russian naval officer, a member of the defense of Sevastopol, the father of the famous diplomat and publicist Prince Esperovich Ukhtomsky. 
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Biography===
2 CONT
2 CONT He participated in the defense of Sevastopol, in the rank of the microman was a signal officer of the headquarters of [https://ru.wikipedia.org/wiki/%D0%9A%D0%BE%D1%80%D0%BD%D0%B8%D0%BB%D0%BE%D0%B2,_%D0%92%D0%BB%D0%B0%D0%B4%D0%B8%
2 CONC D0%BC%D0%B8%D1%80_%D0%90%D0%BB%D0%B5%D0%BA%D1%81%D0%B5%D0%B5%D0%B2%D0%B8%D1%87 '''B. Au. Cornelova''']. He served as an adjutant of Grand Duke [https://ru.wikipedia.org/wiki/%D0%9A%D0%BE%D0%BD%D1%81%D1%82%D0%B0%D0%BD%D1%82%D0%B8%D
2 CONC 0%BD_%D0%9D%D0%B8%D0%BA%D0%BE%D0%BB%D0%B0%D0%B5%D0%B2%D0%B8%D1%87 '''Konstantin Nikolaevich'''].
2 CONT
2 CONT Participated in around-the-world journey on the corvette Vityaz and the campaign I. C. Unkovsky on the frigate  Askold to Japan (hospoused by a senior officer).
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1870 he was captain of the 1st rank.
2 CONT
2 CONT From 1881 he served as an assistant to a naval agent in Austria and Italy.
2 CONT
2 CONT One of the founders of the Russian Insurance Society and the Association of the Russian Eastern Shipping Company, which operated flights to India and China.
2 CONT
2 CONT Died in Vevey, Switzerland from the konakhotka on 21 April 1885. He was buried in the cemetery of Saint-Martin.
2 CONT Family
2 CONT
2 CONT He was married to Eugene (Jenny) Alekseevna Graig (1835-1870), the daughter of the famous Admiral [https://ru.wikipedia.org/wiki/%D0%93%D1%80%D0%B5%D0%B9%D0%B3,_%D0%90%D0%BB%D0%B5%D0%BA%D1%81%D0%B5%D0%B9_%D0%A1%D0%B0%D0%BC%D1%83%D
2 CONC 0%B8%D0%BB%D0%BE%D0%B2%D0%B8%D1%87 '''Alexei Samuilovych Graig''']. Their son is the famous orientalist, diplomat and publicist Prince [https://ru.wikipedia.org/wiki/%D0%A3%D1%85%D1%82%D0%BE%D0%BC%D1%81%D0%BA%D0%B8%D0%B9,_%D0%AD%D
2 CONC 1%81%D0%BF%D0%B5%D1%80_%D0%AD%D1%81%D0%BF%D0%B5%D1%80%D0%BE%D0%B2%D0%B8%D1%87 '''Esper Esperovich Ukhtomsky'''].
